1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,030
A commit is one saved snapshot of a
project, and a run is one execution of a

2
00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:14,082
workflow file on GitHub Actions. If you can
answer that question for any run, from a

3
00:00:14,122 --> 00:00:18,291
workflow file and a description of
the event, you're ready for Gate 7.

4
00:00:18,331 --> 00:00:23,222
If your first instinct is still to ask for
the log, do the exercises at the end of

5
00:00:23,262 --> 00:00:24,245
this video first.

6
00:00:24,585 --> 00:00:28,461
Gate 7 gives you workflow files,
described runs and Git evidence.

7
00:00:28,501 --> 00:00:31,895
It gives you no GitHub, no run
page and no log to scroll.

8
00:00:31,935 --> 00:00:36,595
Everything you need has to come from
reading the file in order and knowing the

9
00:00:36,635 --> 00:00:40,511
defaults. Keep the question on the
slide. The warm-up answers it.

10
00:00:42,351 --> 00:00:45,968
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:46,008 --> 00:00:50,165
This is the briefing for the Actions
gate, the mastery test of Part 6.

12
00:00:50,205 --> 00:00:55,201
It tells you what the gate covers, how its
hands-on part works, which two procedures

13
00:00:55,241 --> 00:00:58,378
to prepare with, and what
form an answer should take.

14
00:00:58,418 --> 00:01:03,355
It doesn't open the gate file or the case
files, and you shouldn't open them before

15
00:01:03,395 --> 00:01:04,374
you sit the gate.

16
00:01:06,214 --> 00:01:07,792
Here's what you'll walk away with.

17
00:01:08,132 --> 00:01:09,544
After this video you can:

18
00:01:09,884 --> 00:01:13,463
state what Gate 7 covers
and its threshold of 85;

19
00:01:13,803 --> 00:01:18,394
explain the paper format of the hands-on
part and the rule never to copy or run its

20
00:01:18,434 --> 00:01:19,232
workflow files;

21
00:01:19,571 --> 00:01:23,461
prepare with the reading order for a
workflow and the investigation order for a

22
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:23,859
failure;

23
00:01:24,199 --> 00:01:28,044
answer every question with the commit,
the ref, the event and the permissions.

24
00:01:29,884 --> 00:01:33,952
What the gate covers. Gate
7 is taken after Module 28.

25
00:01:33,992 --> 00:01:40,342
It covers the workflow model, the first
eleven workflows, runners, and CI debugging.

26
00:01:40,382 --> 00:01:44,755
It passes at 85. Security of
workflows isn't in this gate.

27
00:01:44,795 --> 00:01:46,732
That is Part 7 and Gate 8.

28
00:01:47,072 --> 00:01:52,501
The four parts are the same as in every
gate. Concepts: 30 points, six written

29
00:01:52,541 --> 00:01:57,479
questions, closed book, no terminal.
Prediction: 20 points, four items.

30
00:01:57,519 --> 00:02:02,807
Hands-on diagnosis: 30 points. Oral
interview: 20 points, six questions with

31
00:02:02,847 --> 00:02:08,416
follow-ups, spoken, without notes. The
pass rule is the threshold overall and at

32
00:02:08,456 --> 00:02:10,589
least 70 percent in every part.

33
00:02:10,929 --> 00:02:16,382
The hands-on part is on paper. Three cases,
built from workflow files, described

34
00:02:16,422 --> 00:02:18,235
runs and real Git evidence.

35
00:02:18,575 --> 00:02:22,587
The rule says three things. These
gates run nothing on GitHub.

36
00:02:22,627 --> 00:02:28,075
No GitHub output appears anywhere in them.
And the only gh invocation you need is gh

37
00:02:28,115 --> 00:02:32,976
with a command and --help. Then two
sentences that you must take literally.

38
00:02:33,016 --> 00:02:37,942
The workflow files in these directories
are teaching material with faults on

39
00:02:37,982 --> 00:02:42,058
purpose. Never copy them into a
repository, and never run them.

40
00:02:42,398 --> 00:02:47,357
Why so strict? A file with a deliberate
fault in its trigger, its permissions or its

41
00:02:47,397 --> 00:02:50,689
checkout is the kind of file
Part 7 teaches you to fear.

42
00:02:50,729 --> 00:02:54,199
On paper it teaches. In a
repository with a token, it acts.

43
00:02:54,539 --> 00:02:58,976
What a strong answer contains. A strong
answer names which commit was tested.

44
00:02:59,016 --> 00:03:03,510
More generally, every answer about a run
states four things before it states a

45
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:07,986
finding: the event, the ref and commit,
the permissions of the token, and the

46
00:03:08,026 --> 00:03:09,439
runner. Then the finding.

47
00:03:11,279 --> 00:03:14,765
You prepare with two orders, and
each answers a different question.

48
00:03:15,105 --> 00:03:19,283
The reading order is for a file you
haven't run: trigger, permissions, job and

49
00:03:19,323 --> 00:03:23,771
runner, checkout, commands. It tells you
when a run exists, with what authority, on

50
00:03:23,811 --> 00:03:26,584
what machine, with what
code, and what the check is.

51
00:03:26,924 --> 00:03:32,084
The investigation order is for a run that
happened: workflow, event, permissions,

52
00:03:32,124 --> 00:03:37,477
runner, environment, dependencies, secrets,
action versions, logs, artifacts, cache,

53
00:03:37,517 --> 00:03:38,247
concurrency.

54
00:03:38,587 --> 00:03:44,754
Quick quiz. A paper case has no log. Which
steps of the investigation order can you

55
00:03:44,794 --> 00:03:49,391
still use? A, none. B, the eight
above the log. C, all twelve.

56
00:03:49,431 --> 00:03:50,288
Your answer?

57
00:03:55,128 --> 00:04:00,058
B. The two orders overlap on purpose. The
first four steps of the investigation are

58
00:04:00,098 --> 00:04:05,088
the reading order applied to a concrete run.
In a paper case you have no log at all,

59
00:04:05,128 --> 00:04:09,040
so everything you can conclude
comes from the steps above the log.

60
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:13,950
That's the gate's way of testing whether
you have understood why the log is ninth.

61
00:04:14,290 --> 00:04:17,315
Where this breaks down in
the gate: you can't observe.

62
00:04:17,355 --> 00:04:21,685
On your practice repository you could
check a prediction by looking at a run.

63
00:04:21,725 --> 00:04:25,998
On paper, a claim such as "this job
would be skipped" must be justified by a

64
00:04:26,038 --> 00:04:30,084
documented rule, stated in a sentence.
"I think it would" earns nothing.

65
00:04:31,924 --> 00:04:35,098
Here's the answer frame for
the case you'll replay next.

66
00:04:35,138 --> 00:04:39,518
Four lines first: the event, the ref and
commit, the permissions, the runner.

67
00:04:39,558 --> 00:04:44,281
Line two stays empty for now: you'll fill
it in. Then the finding, the next action,

68
00:04:44,321 --> 00:04:45,544
and what isn't proven.

69
00:04:47,384 --> 00:04:50,428
Step 1: the runner's
checkout for a pull request.

70
00:04:50,768 --> 00:04:52,242
git fetch is 🟢 SAFE.

71
00:04:52,582 --> 00:04:57,719
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds.
Fill in line two of the answer frame from

72
00:04:57,759 --> 00:05:02,503
these two commands alone. Which ref,
how many commits, what kind of HEAD?

73
00:05:04,843 --> 00:05:10,023
One ref, refs/pull/7/merge, one commit,
and a detached HEAD at d04ef31.

74
00:05:10,363 --> 00:05:15,038
The subject of that commit names its two
parents in full: your commit, merged into

75
00:05:15,078 --> 00:05:15,957
the tip of main.

76
00:05:16,297 --> 00:05:18,169
Step 2: what is checked out.

77
00:05:18,509 --> 00:05:22,002
"HEAD (no branch)". The author
of the commit is GitHub.

78
00:05:22,042 --> 00:05:27,397
One commit. No current branch. And the
threshold in the source file is 10, the value

79
00:05:27,437 --> 00:05:28,039
from main.

80
00:05:28,379 --> 00:05:31,883
In a paper case, evidence like
these lines is printed for you.

81
00:05:31,923 --> 00:05:35,427
Your job is to say what it means
for the commands that follow.

82
00:05:35,767 --> 00:05:39,574
Predict the last step. The
author's branch passes its tests.

83
00:05:39,614 --> 00:05:42,588
Will this merge pass them
too? Say it out loud.

84
00:05:47,428 --> 00:05:49,649
Step 3: the tests on the merge.

85
00:05:49,989 --> 00:05:54,980
Exit status 1, one failure. The author's
branch passes, and the merge fails.

86
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:58,190
Both results are correct, and
they tested different commits.

87
00:05:58,530 --> 00:06:03,191
That sentence, with the two commit IDs
in it, is what a case answer needs.

88
00:06:03,231 --> 00:06:07,257
And it answers the opening slide:
the commit tested was d04ef31.

89
00:06:09,097 --> 00:06:10,745
Five mistakes to watch for.

90
00:06:11,085 --> 00:06:14,442
Describing a finding without
saying which commit was tested.

91
00:06:14,482 --> 00:06:19,085
Root cause: for pull_request the run is
about the test merge, so a statement about

92
00:06:19,125 --> 00:06:21,576
"the commit" is ambiguous
until you name it.

93
00:06:21,916 --> 00:06:26,455
Reasoning from a log you do not have. Root
cause: the paper cases contain files,

94
00:06:26,495 --> 00:06:31,034
descriptions and Git evidence; what you
can conclude comes from the steps of the

95
00:06:31,074 --> 00:06:32,579
order that precede the log.

96
00:06:32,919 --> 00:06:37,796
Copying a case's workflow file to try it.
Root cause: those files have faults on

97
00:06:37,836 --> 00:06:40,254
purpose and would run with a real token.

98
00:06:40,594 --> 00:06:45,211
Reading the test command first. Root
cause: whether a run exists, and for which

99
00:06:45,251 --> 00:06:49,278
commit, is decided by the trigger
and the event before any step runs.

100
00:06:49,618 --> 00:06:54,331
Claiming a behavior without the rule behind
it. Root cause: on paper nothing can be

101
00:06:54,371 --> 00:06:57,652
observed, so only a documented
rule supports a prediction.

102
00:06:59,492 --> 00:07:04,102
Now, out of the lab. Picture an incident
review at a company that serves models.

103
00:07:04,142 --> 00:07:07,125
A change reached production
and broke a nightly job.

104
00:07:07,165 --> 00:07:11,718
The pull request had been green. The
question in the review is the one from the

105
00:07:11,758 --> 00:07:14,741
hook: the run was green, so
which commit was tested?

106
00:07:15,081 --> 00:07:18,624
The engineer who answers uses
the frame. Event: pull_request.

107
00:07:18,664 --> 00:07:21,973
Ref and commit: the test merge
made on Tuesday afternoon.

108
00:07:22,013 --> 00:07:26,555
Permissions: read. Runner: hosted. Then
the finding: three merges reached main

109
00:07:26,595 --> 00:07:31,373
between that test merge and the click on
the merge button on Wednesday, and one of

110
00:07:31,413 --> 00:07:36,072
them changed a function the pull request
calls. And then the control: the branch

111
00:07:36,112 --> 00:07:38,011
wasn't required to be up to date.

112
00:07:38,351 --> 00:07:42,839
Nobody in the room needs the log. The
review ends with a decision about a rule,

113
00:07:42,879 --> 00:07:44,559
which is what a review is for.

114
00:07:46,399 --> 00:07:53,035
Your turn. Redo four exercises in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

115
00:07:53,075 --> 00:08:00,175
Exercise 26.6, "Caches and artifacts".
Exercise 27.2, "A deployment with five

116
00:08:00,215 --> 00:08:05,553
flaws". Exercise 28.5, "Green
here, red in a fresh clone".

117
00:08:05,593 --> 00:08:10,281
And Exercise 28.7, "Every
pull request is waiting".

118
00:08:10,621 --> 00:08:15,400
For each, write the four-line frame before
anything else: event, ref and commit,

119
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:19,436
permissions, runner. Predict in
which line the fault will be found.

120
00:08:19,776 --> 00:08:24,537
When you're ready, take Gate 7:
assessments/gate-7-actions.md.

121
00:08:24,577 --> 00:08:26,705
One sitting, parts in order.

122
00:08:30,545 --> 00:08:35,403
Answer out loud. A strong answer separates
the two green results and asks of each:

123
00:08:35,443 --> 00:08:38,926
which commit? Use what you know
about what a re-run reuses.

124
00:08:38,966 --> 00:08:43,764
Then state precisely what has been proven
and about which combination of head and

125
00:08:43,804 --> 00:08:48,781
base, and what hasn't. For the second half,
name the controls that close the gap and

126
00:08:48,821 --> 00:08:53,798
what each costs. The follow-up adopts a
merge queue to close that gap, and the queue

127
00:08:53,838 --> 00:08:58,636
then stalls with every entry waiting for
a check. You met that in the last video.

128
00:09:00,476 --> 00:09:01,403
Let's land this.

129
00:09:01,743 --> 00:09:03,403
You should now be able to say:

130
00:09:03,743 --> 00:09:08,837
Gate 7 covers the workflow model, the
first eleven workflows, runners and CI

131
00:09:08,877 --> 00:09:13,363
debugging, and passes at 85 with
at least 70 percent in every part.

132
00:09:13,703 --> 00:09:18,639
Its hands-on part is three cases on paper;
the workflow files there have faults on

133
00:09:18,679 --> 00:09:20,823
purpose and are never copied or run.

134
00:09:21,163 --> 00:09:25,509
You prepare with the reading order for a
file and the investigation order for a run.

135
00:09:25,849 --> 00:09:29,927
Every answer states the event, the ref and
commit, the permissions and the runner,

136
00:09:29,967 --> 00:09:33,091
and then the finding, including
what the result does not prove.

137
00:09:34,931 --> 00:09:39,829
Before the gate: answer the "Interview
